.TH SUDOVIM 1 "January 2026" "sudovim" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sudovim \- edit and track root-owned configuration files via XDG data directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudovim
[\fI\,OPTION\/\fR]... [\fI\,FILE\/\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sudovim
is a small utility designed to simplify keeping track of which system
configuration files have been edited.

The program acts as a frontend to running 
.I $EDITOR
as root, but additionally creates symbolic link pointing to configuration file
if it has changed.
The symbolic link mirrors the absolute path of the configuration file under
.I $XDG_DATA_DIR/sudovim
\&.

This approach allows users to easily identify and manage which system
configuration files have been modified from their defaults.

.B sudovim
hashes the file and measures it's size before and after editing to determine if
the file has changed. It uses the fast xxhash64 algorithm to reduce hash times.

.SH OPERATION
When invoked on one or more files, sudovim:
.IP "1."
Launches the editor specified by the
.B EDITOR
environment variable with elevated privileges.
.IP "2."
Creates a mirrored directory structure under
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME/sudovim
corresponding to the original file path.
.IP "3."
Creates a symbolic link to the original system file.

For example, running:
.PP
.RS
$ sudovim /etc/default/grub
.RE
.PP
results in:
.PP
.RS
$XDG_DATA_HOME/sudovim/etc/default/grub -> /etc/default/grub 
.RE
only if the file has been changed.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
List tracked configuration files under
.BR \$XDG_DATA_HOME/sudovim
and exit.

This option is processed before any file arguments.
All remaining arguments are ignored when
.B \-l
is specified.
At present, sudovim does not define command-line options beyond specifying
one or more file paths to edit.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/sudovim/
Root directory where tracked configuration files are stored.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Specifies the text editor to use when opening files.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for application data.
Defaults to
.I ~/.local/share
if unset.

.SH NOTES
Files created dynamically within the editor session (for example via
editor commands that open new, unnamed files) may not be automatically
tracked until they are saved explicitly.

sudovim is intended as a lightweight helper for configuration management,
not a full replacement for version control systems.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vim (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR xdg-base-directory (7)

